{{!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     navbar/brand — the mark and the site name, as one link home.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------
     ALWAYS BOTH. The bar shows a square mark AND the site title as text, and
     the title is never swapped out for an image: a bar whose whole identity
     is a picture loses the name from the accessibility tree, from a text
     browser, and from the moment the image 404s.

     ── @site.icon FIRST, AND THIS MATTERS ───────────────────────────────────
     Ghost has two branding images and they are different shapes. @site.icon
     is the SQUARE mark; @site.logo is a WORDMARK, usually much wider than it
     is tall. NSDS sets

         .ns-topnav__brand img { inline-size: 1.5rem; block-size: 1.5rem; }

     — both axes, no object-fit — so whatever lands in this slot is forced
     into a 24px square. A wordmark put here does not scale down, it gets
     squashed. So the icon is preferred and the logo is only a fallback for a
     site that has set one and not the other.

     The full wordmark has a home already: the homepage hero, where it has the
     width to be itself (partials/home/hero.hbs).

     alt="" is correct and deliberate — the name is right beside it inside the
     same link, so a real alt would announce the brand twice.
     ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ --}}
<a class="ns-topnav__brand" href="{{@site.url}}">
    {{#if @site.icon}}
        <img src="{{img_url @site.icon size="xs"}}" alt="">
    {{else if @site.logo}}
        <img src="{{img_url @site.logo size="xs"}}" alt="">
    {{/if}}
    <span class="ns-topnav__brand-name">{{@site.title}}</span>
</a>
